## 4. Every exact phase retains more than `0.7035D`

For one phase retained mass `s`, write

\[
f(s)=s-2\sin(s/2).
\]

The phasewise one-well bathtub theorem gives

\[
\delta\ge f(z_C)+f(z_U)+f(z_V)-o(1).
\tag{E645.18}
\]

Also, from (E645.3) and `x,h>=0`,

\[
\delta\le {R_T\over2}+o(1).
\tag{E645.19}
\]

Suppose one phase has retained mass at most

\[
A_0=0.7035<Z_0/3.
\]

For fixed total mass at least `Z_0`, strict convexity makes the cheapest other two masses equal, so

\[
f(z_C)+f(z_U)+f(z_V)
\ge
f(A_0)+2f\left({Z_0-A_0\over2}\right)-o(1).
\]

The exact certificate proves this lower bound exceeds `R_T/2` by more than

\[
1.05\times10^{-6}.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{z_C,z_U,z_V>0.7035-o(1).}
\tag{E645.20}
\]

---

## 5. Two good leaf O--O families force the final energy contradiction

For either leaf phase `i`, retained terminal weights satisfy

\[
z_i\le M_i+P_i+o(1),
\]

where `M_i` is the phase train mass and `P_i` the retained O--O pair weight.  Combining (E645.11) and (E645.20),

\[
\boxed{P_U,P_V>0.1735-o(1).}
\tag{E645.21}
\]

Call a leaf O--O train bad if it crosses at most half of the center-phase trains.  The hostile-audited center/leaf correlation bounds total bad retained pair weight across both leaves by the quadratic whose safe test value is

\[
B=0.037.
\]

With `W_0,E_0,U_0` above, the corresponding discriminant is

\[
-0.001643515004<0.
\tag{E645.22}
\]

so total bad weight is below `0.037D`.  Even if all of it is charged to one leaf, each leaf retains good O--O weight

\[
\boxed{G_U,G_V>0.1365-o(1).}
\tag{E645.23}
\]

Every good train crosses more than half of the same center family.  Hence any good `U`-train and good `V`-train share a center neighbor.  The stopped-train crossing graph is triangle-free, so the two leaf trains cannot cross.

The scope-repaired cross-phase endpoint theorem now applies to every good cross-product pair:

\[
e_\tau+e_\sigma\ge2-\sqrt3-o(1).
\tag{E645.24}
\]

Summing the weighted complete product yields

\[
E_U+E_V
\ge(2-\sqrt3)(0.1365)-o(1).
\tag{E645.25}
\]

The center selected-edge population is disjoint and, by (E645.20), contributes at least `f(0.7035)D-o(D)` additional outer energy.  Therefore

\[
\delta
\ge
(2-\sqrt3)(0.1365)+f(0.7035)-o(1).
\tag{E645.26}
\]

The exact certificate proves

\[
(2-\sqrt3)(0.1365)+f(0.7035)
>{R_T\over2}+5.43\times10^{-4}.
\tag{E645.27}
\]

This contradicts (E645.19).  Hence no scoped full-core Type-II survivor has `t<=1.843645`, proving (E645.1).

---

## 6. Numerical ceiling and next obligation

A non-load-bearing continuous optimization of this exact architecture puts its limiting endpoint near

\[
t\approx1.84365114.
\]

The present theorem deliberately stops at `1.843645`; the remaining margin is large enough for exact outward rounding, while attempting `1.843651` would leave only a few `10^{-5}` in the final relaxed energy ledger.

This shows the two-leaf scalar architecture is nearly exhausted.  Further useful progress should come from the newly merged adjacent-cut / multishear finite-cusp structure rather than another decimal push.  In particular, the live `t<=1.844` branch now supplies adjacent phase-close O--O cuts whose opposite multishear has only a bounded switching set of `O(D)` first-derivative diameter rows.  The current audit correctly warns that KKT positivity forbids a nonzero first-order feasible direction, so the next theorem must use the finite switching set combinatorially or build a nonlocal same-cardinality replacement.

Run

```sh
python3 research/verify_target_c_two_leaf_outer_energy_exclusion_t1843645.py
```

for the exact rational/Machin/Taylor certificate.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only full-core Type-II strengthening. C-II and Level I remain open.
